:"TB","home":false,"pf":26.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":73,"epa_per_play":-0.132,"pass_rate":0.7123,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6452,"def_epa_per_play":-0.252},{"week":7,"opponent":"NO","home":false,"pf":16.0,"pa":10.0,"off_snaps":61,"epa_per_play":0.1158,"pass_rate":0.541,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6111,"def_epa_per_play":0.05605},{"week":8,"opponent":"CLE","home":true,"pf":27.0,"pa":6.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":0.1264,"pass_rate":0.431,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4706,"def_epa_per_play":-0.2093},{"week":10,"opponent":"CIN","home":false,"pf":18.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":0.02967,"pass_rate":0.6207,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6667,"def_epa_per_play":0.3492},{"week":11,"opponent":"PHI","home":false,"pf":16.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":69,"epa_per_play":-0.1283,"pass_rate":0.7101,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6522,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1479}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"SEA","distance":0.5921,"shared":12,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"DEN","distance":0.683,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Sean Payton"},{"season":2023,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7922,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Zac Taylor"},{"season":2025,"team":"KC","distance":0.8211,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Andy Reid"},{"season":2025,"team":"TEN","distance":0.8533,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Brian Callahan"}],"defense":[{"season":2024,"team":"LA","distance":0.08655,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Chris Shula"},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.2732,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2022,"team":"DAL","distance":0.2802,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Dan Quinn"},{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":0.294,"shared":4,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"},{"season":2025,"team":"BUF","distance":0.2978,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Steelers are 6-4 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have outscored opponents 214 to 201. The defense has done most of the work, ranking 11th in EPA allowed while the offense sits 21st in EPA per play. Their most distinctive trait is the 2nd highest screen rate in the league.","offense":"The Steelers offense has been a little below average by EPA per play so far, and it has been much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd highest screen rate and the lowest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Aaron Rodgers has run the passing game, with DK Metcalf leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Steelers defense has been a little above average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 3rd and 15th. Alex Highsmith has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Patrick Queen, who has 3.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 12th highest in the league.","special":"The Steelers have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 81% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 31% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Aaron Rodgers has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,241 yards. Targets have been spread around, with DK Metcalf and Germie Bernard leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. Jaylen Warren has handled most of the work in the backfield with 63% of the carries. On defense, T.J. Watt leads the team with 7.5 sacks.","coaching":"Mike McCarthy is calling the offense as head coach and Patrick Graham is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2024 Seahawks and the 2025 Broncos under Sean Payton. Their defense looks most like the 2024 Rams under Chris Shula and the 2024 Bengals under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 16th in EPA per play in 2025 to 21st so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 18th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 11th so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was two point attempt rate, which went from 4% to 14%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}